<section>
<h3>The do-operator: seeing is not doing</h3>
<p>Here is the conceptual hinge of the entire modern field, and it is worth saying slowly. There are two very different things you can do with a variable X.</p>
<p>You can <strong>condition</strong>{% tip 'Conditioning means narrowing attention to cases where some variable already has a given value.' %} on it — written <code>P(Y | X = x)</code>. This means: among all the cases where X <em>happened</em> to equal x, what's the distribution of Y? You're filtering an existing population. This is <em>seeing</em>.</p>
<p>Or you can <strong>intervene</strong> — written, in Pearl's notation, <code>P(Y | do(X = x))</code>. This means: reach in, <em>force</em> X to equal x for everyone, snipping X away from whatever usually causes it, and then watch Y. This is <em>doing</em>. In an ideal randomized trial, assignment is designed to approximate that severing operation.</p>
<p>The two may coincide, but equality requires a design or causal assumptions; it does not follow from their numerical similarity. <code>P(Y | X=x)</code> describes outcomes among cases in which X was observed to equal x. <code>P(Y | do(X=x))</code> describes outcomes in a hypothetical regime that sets X to x. The machine below makes the difference auditable by calculating both quantities from an explicit structural model.</p>
</section>

<div class="aside">
<p class="h">The potential-outcomes lens</p>
<p>The other major language of modern causal inference writes the same idea at the level of units. For unit <code>i</code>, <em class="term">potential outcomes</em>{% tip 'Potential outcomes are the outcomes a unit would have under different treatment or intervention values, even though only one is observed.' %} are <code>Y<sub>i</sub>(1)</code> and <code>Y<sub>i</sub>(0)</code>: the outcome if treated and the outcome if untreated. A common <em class="term">causal estimand</em>{% tip 'An estimand is the precise causal quantity a study is trying to learn, such as an average treatment effect.' %} is the average treatment effect, <code>ATE = E[Y(1)-Y(0)]</code>. The catch is the fundamental problem of causal inference: for any one unit, only one of those two potential outcomes is observed. The other is counterfactual.</p>
<p>Designs and assumptions connect this target to data. <em class="term">Consistency</em>{% tip 'Consistency says a unit observed under treatment value x has the same outcome as its potential outcome under x.' %} links observed outcomes to potential outcomes under the treatment actually received. <em class="term">Exchangeability</em>{% tip 'Exchangeability says treated and untreated groups are comparable with respect to the relevant potential outcomes, possibly after conditioning on covariates.' %} says the groups being compared are comparable with respect to the relevant potential outcomes. <em class="term">Positivity</em>{% tip 'Positivity, or overlap, means each relevant covariate group has a nonzero chance of receiving each treatment level being compared.' %} means every relevant covariate group has a chance of receiving each treatment level. <em class="term">No interference</em>{% tip 'No interference means one unit’s treatment does not change another unit’s outcome, unless such spillovers are explicitly modeled.' %} means one unit's treatment does not alter another unit's outcome, unless spillovers are part of the model.</p>
<p>A safe causal workflow is: specify the unit, exposure, outcome, timing, and intervention; define the estimand; state the causal assumptions; determine whether the estimand is identified; estimate it statistically; then assess uncertainty, measurement, attrition, selection, overlap, and sensitivity to violations.</p>
</div>

