<div class="format-alt epub-only print-only">
<p class="ptitle">Worked example</p>
<h3>Seeing Is Not Doing</h3>
<p>In the default toy structural model, rain makes umbrellas more common and wet clothes more common. Umbrellas reduce wetness, but umbrella users are wetter in the observed data because they are disproportionately standing in rain.</p>
<table class="alt-table">
<thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>What it asks</th><th>Default reading</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>P(wet | umbrella) - P(wet | no umbrella)</code></td><td>Seeing: compare existing umbrella users with non-users.</td><td>+0.23 observed association.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>P(wet | do(umbrella)) - P(wet | do(no umbrella))</code></td><td>Doing: set umbrella use directly and sever its usual causes.</td><td>−0.16 causal effect.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Back-door adjusted contrast</td><td>Average the rain-specific umbrella contrasts using the population rain mix.</td><td>−0.16, matching the intervention contrast under this model.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Formula: <code>P(Y=1 | do(X=x)) = Σ<sub>s</sub> P(Y=1 | X=x,S=s)P(S=s)</code>. The observed contrast instead uses <code>P(S=s | X=x)</code>, which changes when rain affects umbrella use.</p>
</div>

<section>
<p>That severed arrow in the right-hand graph is the do-operator made visual. Intervening doesn't just <em>look</em> at X — it <strong>deletes the arrows pointing into X</strong> and replaces them with your hand. The rain→umbrella link is cut, so rain can no longer use umbrella use as a backdoor route to distort the umbrella→wetness contrast. Under this graph, the back-door-adjusted contrast recovers the intervention contrast.</p>
<p>Pearl gave us a grammar for doing this on paper. The recursive or acyclic <em class="term">structural causal models</em>{% tip 'A structural causal model represents how variables are generated from other variables and from background noise.' %} used in this lesson are represented by <em class="term">directed acyclic graphs</em>{% tip 'A directed acyclic graph, or DAG, is a network of arrows with no directed loops.' %}: boxes and arrows with no directed loops. Cyclic and dynamic structural models also exist. In a DAG, a <em class="term">fork</em> (X ← Z → Y) can be a confounder; a <em class="term">chain</em> (X → Z → Y) can be a mediator; and a <em class="term">collider</em> (X → Z ← Y) is the trap. A path that enters a node through two arrowheads is normally closed at that collider. Conditioning on the collider — or sometimes one of its descendants — can open that path and induce an association along that route. This is why "controlling for everything you can measure" is not cautious but reckless.</p>
